WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.372
To a client, a load-balanced service can look
like one powerful server: it has one familiar

00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:10.627
interface and accepts requests at a stable entry
point. Behind that interface, a balancer

00:00:10.627 --> 00:00:14.582
selects one of several application workers and
relays the exchange.

00:00:14.582 --> 00:00:19.575
The workers perform the expensive application
work; the balancer mainly distributes and

00:00:19.575 --> 00:00:24.453
forwards it. This example makes the apparent
single service expand into three workers

00:00:24.453 --> 00:00:29.733
without changing the client's request format.
This is useful only if the selected worker can

00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:30.881
handle that request.

00:00:31.082 --> 00:00:36.170
If a session or unfinished operation lives
exclusively in another worker's memory, routing

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:40.297
to a healthy replica may still produce an
incorrect or incomplete result.

00:00:40.297 --> 00:00:45.844
Distributing equal numbers of requests does not
necessarily distribute equal work. Suppose

00:00:45.844 --> 00:00:51.390
round robin sends one short metadata lookup to
worker A, one large export to worker B, and

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:56.875
another lookup to worker C. Each has received
one request, but B may remain occupied long

00:00:56.875 --> 00:00:58.354
after the others finish.

00:00:58.354 --> 00:01:03.370
This example assigns different costs to equal
request counts. Alternatives such as least-

00:01:03.370 --> 00:01:07.765
connections or weighted routing use other
signals, but those signals are still

00:01:07.765 --> 00:01:12.442
approximations of useful available capacity. A
persistent connection can carry very

00:01:12.442 --> 00:01:13.907
different amounts of work.

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:19.010
Choose a policy based on the service's workload
and observe completion latency, queueing,

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:23.425
and utilization rather than congratulating the
system merely for even counts.

00:01:23.425 --> 00:01:28.675
A layer-four balancer makes routing decisions
from transport information such as addresses,

00:01:28.675 --> 00:01:33.405
ports, and connection state. In a NAT-based
implementation, it rewrites addressing

00:01:33.405 --> 00:01:38.482
information and preserves a mapping so packets
belonging to a connection continue to the

00:01:38.482 --> 00:01:39.290
chosen worker.

00:01:39.425 --> 00:01:43.895
The return path uses that mapping to make the
response appear to come from the public

00:01:43.895 --> 00:01:48.576
service. Follow one connection through the
address translation instead of pretending that

00:01:48.576 --> 00:01:51.837
every packet is independently assigned to an
arbitrary worker.

00:01:51.925 --> 00:01:57.209
This approach can support protocols beyond HTTP
because it does not need to understand

00:01:57.209 --> 00:02:02.861
application resource names. NAT is one
implementation technique, however, not the
definition

00:02:02.861 --> 00:02:04.950
of every layer-four load balancer.

00:02:04.950 --> 00:02:10.404
An HTTP-aware reverse proxy operates at a
different boundary. It can inspect a request
path

00:02:10.404 --> 00:02:15.379
and route image requests to one pool while
sending account requests to another. For

00:02:15.379 --> 00:02:20.773
encrypted HTTP traffic, the proxy must terminate
the incoming TLS connection before it can

00:02:20.773 --> 00:02:25.928
inspect the HTTP message; it may then establish
a separate encrypted connection to the

00:02:25.928 --> 00:02:27.007
selected upstream.

00:02:27.007 --> 00:02:32.523
In this example, notice the path-based decision
after TLS termination, with two possible

00:02:32.523 --> 00:02:37.788
destinations. That extra understanding enables
features such as application routing,

00:02:37.788 --> 00:02:43.367
compression, and eligible response caching, but
also creates configuration and processing

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:48.946
work. The proxy must preserve the intended
authentication, forwarding, and cache behavior

00:02:48.946 --> 00:02:50.199
across the boundary.
